#etcsl#alt t1814.v23 t1814.v24
#etcsl#alt t1814.v25 t1814.v26
#etcsl#altGrpc

@(1.184 = 184-198) @q{“You should not put on your clean garments: they would recognise immediately that you are alien. You should not anoint yourself with fine oil from a bowl: they would surround you at  @varh[t1814.v27]{its }@vart[t1814.v28,t1814.v27]{@varn{1 ms. has instead:} your } scent. You should not hurl throw-sticks in the nether world: those struck down by the throw-sticks would surround you. You should not not hold a cornel-wood stick in your hand: the spirits would feel insulted by you. You should not put sandals on your feet. You should not shout in the nether world. You should not kiss your beloved wife. You should not hit your wife even if you are annoyed with her. You should not kiss your beloved child. You should not hit your son even if you are annoyed with him. The outcry aroused would detain you in the nether world.”}

#etcsl#altGrpo
#etcsl#alt t1814.v27 t1814.v28
#etcsl#altGrpc

@(1.199 = 199-204) @q{“She who lies there, she who lies there, @wDN{Ninazu's} mother who lies there -- her pure shoulders are not covered with a garment, and no linen is spread over her pure breast. She has fingers like a pickaxe, she plucks her hair out like leeks.”}

#etcsl#linkGrpo
#etcsl#link type=q t1814.q7 t1814.q8 t1814.q9
#etcsl#linkGrpc

@(1.205 = 205-220) @wPN{Enkidu}, however, did not heed not his master's words. He put on his clean garments and they recognised that he was alien. He anointed himself with fine oil from a bowl and they surrounded him at its scent. He hurled throw-sticks in the nether world and those struck down by the throw-sticks surrounded him. He held a cornel-wood stick in his hand and the spirits felt insulted by him. He put sandals on his feet. He caused irritation in the nether world. He kissed his beloved wife and hit his wife when he was annoyed with her. He kissed his beloved child and hit his son when he was annoyed with him. He aroused an outcry and was detained in the nether world.

@(1.221 = 221-229) The warrior @wDN{Gilgameš}, son of @wDN{Ninsumun}, directed his steps on his own to @wTN{E-kur}, the temple of @wDN{Enlil}. He cried before @wDN{Enlil}: @q[Gilgamec,Enlil]{“Father @wDN{Enlil}, my ball (?) fell down into the nether world, my mallet (?) fell down into @wGN{Ganzer}. @wPN{Enkidu} went down to retrieve them but the nether world has seized him. @wDN{Namtar} did not seize him, the Asag did not seize him; but the nether world has seized him. The @fsux{udug} demon of @wDN{Nergal}, who spares nobody, did not seize him, but the nether world has seized him. He did not fall in battle on the field of manhood, but the nether world has seized him.”} Father @wDN{Enlil} did not stand by him in the matter, so he went to @wSN{Eridug}.

@(1.230 = 230-237) In @wSN{Eridug} he directed his steps on his own to the temple of @wDN{Enki}. He cried before @wDN{Enki}: @q[Gilgamec,Enki]{“Father @wDN{Enki}, my ball (?) fell down into the nether world, my mallet (?) fell down into @wGN{Ganzer}. @wPN{Enkidu} went down to retrieve them but the nether world has seized him. @wDN{Namtar} did not seize him, the Asag did not seize him; but the nether world has seized him. The @fsux{udug} demon of @wDN{Nergal}, who spares nobody, did not seize him, but the nether world has seized him. He did not fall in battle on the field of manhood, but the nether world has seized him.”} Father @wDN{Enki} stood by him in this matter.

@(1.238 = 238-242) He said to the young warrior @wDN{Utu}, the son born by @wDN{Ningal}: @q[Enki,Utu]{“Open a hole in the nether world immediately, and then bring up his servant from the nether world!”} He opened a hole in the nether world and brought up his servant with his breeze (?) from the nether world.

@(1.243 = 243-253) They hugged and kissed. They wearied each other with questions: @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the order of the nether world? -- If only you would tell me, my friend, if only you would tell me!”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“If I tell you the order of the nether world, sit down and weep! I shall sit down and weep! ……, which your heart rejoiced to touch, is ……, worms infest it like an old garment (?); like …… of (?) a crevice, it is full of dust.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Alas!”} he said and sat down in the dust.

@(1.254 = 254-267) @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had one son?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He weeps bitterly at the wooden peg which was driven into his wall.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had two sons?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He sits on a couple of bricks, eating bread.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had three sons?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He drinks water from a saddle waterskin.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had four sons?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“His heart rejoices like a man who has four asses to yoke.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had five sons?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“Like a good scribe he is indefatigable, he enters the palace easily.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had six sons?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He is a cheerful as a ploughman.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had seven sons?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“As a companion of the gods, he sits on a throne and listens to judgments.”}

@(1.268 = 268-285) @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the palace eunuch?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“Like a useless @fsux{alala} stick he is propped in a corner.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the woman who never gave birth?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw her.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does she fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“Like a …… pot, she is thrown away violently, she gives no man joy.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the young man who never undressed his wife?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“You finish a rope, and he weeps over the rope.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the young woman who never undressed her husband?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw her.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does she fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“You finish a reed mat, and she weeps over the reed mat.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had no heir?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“Like him who …… bricks (?), he eats bread.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“……?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”}  @gap{7 lines fragmentary or missing} 

@(1.286 = 286-303) @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see ……?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“His food is set apart, his water is set apart, he eats the food offered (?) to him, he drinks the water offered (?) to him.”}  @vart[t1814.v29,t1814.v29]{@varn{1 ms. adds:} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who was eaten by a lion?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He cries bitterly @q{“O my hands! O my legs!”}”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who fell down from the roof?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“They cannot …… his bones.”} } @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the leprous man?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He twitches like an ox as the worms eat at him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who fell in battle?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“His father and mother are not there to hold his head, and his wife weeps.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the spirit of him who has no funerary offerings?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He eats the scraps and the crumbs …… tossed out in the street.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him hit by a ship's board  @vart[t1814.v30,t1814.v30]{@varn{1 ms. adds:} when diving (?) }? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“@q{“Alas, my mother!”} the man cries to her, as he pulls out the ship's board ……, he …… cross beam …… crumbs.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see my little stillborn children who never knew existence?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw them.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How do they fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“They play at a table of gold and silver, laden with honey and ghee.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who died ……?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He lies on a bed of the gods.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who was set on fire?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I did not see him. His spirit is not about. His smoke went up to the sky.”}



@h1 A version from Urim (UET 6 58)

@(2.1 = 1-7) @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who fell down from the roof?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“They cannot …… his bones.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who was struck in (?) a flood-storm of (?) @wDN{Iškur}?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He twitches like an ox as the worms eat at him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the leprous man?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“His food is set apart, his water is set apart, he eats the food offered (?) to him, he drinks the water offered (?) to him. He lives outside the city.”}

@(2.8 = 8-19) @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had no respect for the word of his mother and father?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“@q{“O my body! O my limbs!”} he never ceases to cry.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who was reached by the curse of his mother and father?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He is deprived of an heir. His spirit roams about.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who …… the name of his god?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“His spirit …….”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the spirit of him who has no funerary offerings?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He eats the scraps and the crumbs …… tossed out in the street.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see my little stillborn children who never knew existence?”}@q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw them.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How do they fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“They play at a table of gold and silver, laden with honey and ghee.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who was set on fire?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I did not see him. His smoke went up to the sky. His spirit does not live in the underworld.”}

@(2.20 = 20-28) @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who lied to the gods while swearing an oath?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He drinks …… which has been drunk …… the libation place at the entrance (?) to the nether world.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the citizen of @wSN{Ŋirsu} who refused (?) water to his father and his mother?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“In front of each of them are a thousand @wEN{Martu}, and his spirit can neither …… nor ……. The @wEN{Martu} at the libation place at the entrance (?) to the nether world …….”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the citizens of @wGN{Sumer} and @wGN{Akkad}?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw them.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How do they fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“They drink the water of the …… place, muddy water.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see where my father and my mother live?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I saw them.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“How do they fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“Both of them drink the water of the …… place, muddy water.”}



@h1 Another version from Urim (UET 6 59)


@(3.A.1 = 1-9) @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him hit by a ship's board? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“@q{“Alas, my mother!”} the man cries to her, as he pulls out ……, he …… crossbeam …… crumbs.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who fell down from the roof? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He twitches like an ox as the worms eat at him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who was reached by the curse of his mother? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He is deprived of an heir. His spirit roams (?) about.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had no respect for the word of his father and his mother? How does he fare?”}  @gap{1 line fragmentary}   @gap{unknown no. of lines missing} 


@(3.B.1 = 1-11) @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“His food is set apart, his water is set apart, he eats the food offered (?) to him, he drinks the water offered (?) to him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who fell in battle? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“His father and mother are not there to hold his head, and his wife weeps.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who ……? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“…… from his (?) hand …….”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the spirit of him who has no funerary offerings? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He eats the scraps and the crumbs tossed out in the street.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see my little stillborn children who never knew existence? How do they fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“They play with a bucket of gold and silver, full of honey and ghee.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who was set on fire?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“I did not see him. His spirit is not there. His smoke went up to the sky.”}



@h1 A third version from Urim (UET 6 60)

@(4.1 = 1-10) They returned to @wSN{Unug}, they returned to their city. He entered outfitted with tools and armaments, with an axe and a spear, and deposited them in his palace happily. Looking at the statue, the young men and women of @wSN{Unug} and the old men (?) and women of @wSN{Kulaba} rejoiced. As @wDN{Utu} came forth from his bedchamber, @wDN{Gilgameš} (?) raised his head and told them (?): @q[Gilgamec,]{“My father and my mother, drink clean water!”} Midday had hardly passed when they touched the statue's (?) crown.

@(4.11 = 11-16) @wDN{Gilgameš} threw himself down at the place of mourning, he threw himself down for nine days at the place of mourning. The young men and women of @wSN{Unug} and the old men (?) and women of @wSN{Kulaba} wept. As soon as he had said that, he repulsed the citizen of @wSN{Ŋirsu}. @q[Gilgamec,]{“My father and my mother, drink clean water!”}

@(4.17 = 17) Warrior @wDN{Gilgameš}, son of @wDN{Ninsumun}, sweet is your praise!



@h1 A version from Me-Turan


@(5.A.1 = 1-9) …… surrounded him. He carried …… and the spirits felt insulted (?) by him. He caused (?) …….  @gap{1 line fragmentary}  He kissed his beloved wife, and hit his wife when he was angry with her. He kissed his beloved child, and hit his son when he was angry with him. He aroused an outcry and was detained in the nether world.

@(5.A.10 = 10-16) From that fateful day and for seven days his servant, @wPN{Enkidu}, did not come out from the nether world. The king was lamenting, crying bitterly: @q[Gilgamec,]{“My beloved servant, my faithful companion, my counsellor, has been seized in the nether world! @wDN{Namtar} did not seize him, the Asag did not seize him; but he was seized in the nether world. The @fsux{udug} of @wDN{Nergal} who …… did not seize him, but he was seized in the nether world. He did not fall in battle on the field of ……, but he was seized in the nether world.”}

@(5.A.17 = 17-24) He directed his steps on his own to @wTN{E-kur}, the temple of @wDN{Enlil}. Before @wDN{Enlil}, he ……: @q[Gilgamec,Enlil]{“My ball (?) fell down into the nether world, my mallet (?) fell down into @wGN{Ganzer}. But @wPN{Enkidu}, going down to retrieve them, my beloved servant, my faithful companion, my counsellor, was seized in the nether world. @wDN{Namtar} did not seize him, the Asag did not seize him, but he was seized in the nether world. …… did not seize him, but he was seized in the nether world.”}  @gap{unknown no. of lines missing} 


@(5.B.1 = 1-28) @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had one son? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He weeps bitterly …….”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had two sons? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He sits on …….”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had three sons? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He drinks water …….”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had four sons? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“His heart is happy  @vart[t1814.v31,t1814.v31]{@varn{1 ms. adds:} like a man who has four asses to yoke }.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had five sons? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“Like a good scribe he is indefatigable, he enters the palace easily.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had six sons? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He is cheerful as a ploughman.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had seven sons? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“As a companion of the gods he sits on a throne and listens to judgments.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who had no heir? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“Like (?) …… he eats bread.”}  @gap{approx. 3 lines missing} 

@(5.B.29 = 29-51) @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him ……? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He drinks water …….”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him ……? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He …… as the worms eat at him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who was eaten by a dog? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He …… @q{“O my hands! O my legs! O ……!”}”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him hit (?) by the mast of a boat? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“@q{“Alas, my mother”} the man cries to her, …… wooden peg ……, he …… food, cross beam (?), crumbs …….”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the woman who never gave birth? How does she fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“Like a …… pot, she is thrown away violently, she …… nobody.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the young man who never undressed his wife? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“You finish a reed mat and he weeps over the reed mat.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the young woman who never undressed her husband? How does she fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“You finish a …… garment and she weeps over the …… garment.”}

@(5.B.52 = 52-68) @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who …… extolled himself? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He bows down (?) like an ox as the worms eat at him.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see him who fell down from the roof? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“His bones …… and his spirit …….”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see ……? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“He …….”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see the leprous man? How does he fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“His water is set apart, his food is set apart. He …… the spirits. He lives outside the city.”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Did you see my stillborn children who never received a name? How do they fare?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“They play at a table of gold and silver …….”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“Didn't you see him who was set on fire?”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“Why, my friend, did not you spare this question?”} @q[Gilgamec,Enkidu]{“I asked it, my friend!”} @q[Enkidu,Gilgamec]{“His spirit is …… from the nether world, it went up to the sky with the smoke (?).”}

@(5.B.69 = 69-71) His heart was smitten, his insides were ravaged. The king began to search for life. Now the lord once decided to set off for the mountain where the man lives. @noteref{These three lines create a transition to 1.8.1.5 Gilgameš and Ḫuwawa (Version A).}

